\title{Carmichael's Fibonacci Primitive Divisor Theorem:\\
A Verified Proof on a Bounded Range,\\
with an Unconditional Prime-Index Case}
\author{Aristotle (Harmonic)}
\date{2026-06-15}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{primitive prime divisor} of the Fibonacci number $\F(n)$ is a prime $p$
with $p \mid \F(n)$ but $p \nmid \F(k)$ for all $0 < k < n$. Carmichael's
classical theorem (1913) asserts that $\F(n)$ has a primitive prime divisor for
every index $n$ outside the exceptional set $\{1,2,6,12\}$. We present a fully
rigorous, machine-checked proof of Carmichael's theorem on the range
$13 \le n \le 10000$, assembled from two logically independent pillars. The first
is an \emph{unconditional} structural result: for every prime index $n \ge 3$,
\emph{every} prime factor of $\F(n)$ is primitive. Its proof is a three-line
entry-point argument resting on the strong-divisibility identity
$\gcdop(\F(m),\F(n)) = \F(\gcdop(m,n))$. The second pillar handles composite
indices through a verified ``strip-the-imprimitive-part'' algorithm $\primPart$:
starting from $\F(n)$, it removes every prime shared with a proper-divisor
Fibonacci number, and we prove that a residue exceeding $1$ certifies a primitive
prime divisor. An exhaustive computation confirms the certificate for all
composite $n$ in the range. We discuss why the prime and composite regimes demand
different techniques, why $13$ is the sharp threshold, and how the unbounded
composite tail reduces to a Lifting-the-Exponent law for Fibonacci $p$-adic
valuations, which we state as the principal open formalization target.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} Fibonacci numbers, primitive prime divisors,
Carmichael's theorem, entry point, strong divisibility, lifting the exponent,
formal verification.

